  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1247 PM CST Thu Nov 5 2026\n\nIsolated weak showers are currently moving northward across the \nsouthern portions of the Coastal Plains and Brush Country this \nafternoon. Models indicate an increase in rain chances tonight \ninitially due to a stalled boundary, then a cold front overnight \ninto Friday morning. The highest chances will be across the southern \nportions of S TX with a 40% probability from Corpus Christi to \nEncinal and up to a 70% probability from Kingsville to south of \nLaredo. This activity will be mostly showers with a few \nthunderstorms. Higher chances with heavier rainfall exists across \nDeep S TX. Rain chances drop off from north to south Friday morning \nas drier and cooler air moves into S TX.\n\nA deepening mid/upper level long wave trough is progged to move \ntoward the Desert SW and W TX this weekend. This will bring another \nround of showers and thunderstorms to S TX as embedded short waves \ntrack northeast out ahead of the main trough. The combination of the \nshort waves with a 90KT upper jet, deep moisture (PWATs up to 1.9 \ninches) and low level moisture convergence due to a secondary surge \nof surface high pressure, will result in a high chance for showers \nand thunderstorms Saturday. The surge of high pressure will usher in \na strong, but shallow cold airmass into S TX Saturday night, with \nisentropic upglide developing overhead and maintaining the high \nchance of rain with embedded thunderstorms through Sunday. Rain \nchances slowly decrease from west to east Sunday night through \nMonday as the mid level trough begins to move northeast of the area. \nConfidence in this scenario coming to fruition remains low to \nmoderate due to variations between the model outputs. Timing and \nstrength of this system may change as we draw closer to the weekend, \ndepending on how far south the mid level trough digs west of S TX \nand how fast or slow the trough lifts northeast out of the area. \n\nThe uncertainty with this forecast also makes forecasting rainfall \ntotals tricky, but for now S TX is expected to receive 1-2.5 inches \nfrom Saturday through Monday morning. Isolated higher amounts up to \n3-4 inches are possible. These amounts may lead to flooding, \nespecially low lying areas, streams, creeks, and urban areas. \n\nAs for the temperatures, the cold front moving through the area \novernight will lead to max temperatures in the 70s to around 80 on \nFriday. Highs in the 70s on Saturday will more likely be due to \ncloud cover and rain. The reinforcing surge late Saturday will bring \nhighs in the 60s to around 70 for Sunday and Monday. Lows in the mid \n50s to around 60 along the coastline can be expected by Saturday \nnight with the second reinforcing surge of high pressure. Cooler \ntemps with low to mid 50s (around 60 across the islands) are \nexpected Sunday night\n\nThe chance of minor coastal flooding along gulf facing beaches will \nincrease through Friday and especially by the weekend. This is due \nto a strengthening northeast flow across the gulf waters by Saturday \nwhich is conducive for ekman forcing to push the water up to the \ndunes during high tide. In addition, normal tide levels will also be \non the increase as we head toward a new moon. Latest P-ETSS run \nindicated that tide levels may exceed the 2ft MSL threshold during \ntimes of high tide by Friday with 2.0-2.5 feet MSL over the weekend. \nThe P-ETSS is now showing tide levels approaching 3 feet MSL by \nMonday during high tide, but confidence is low at this time. The one \nthing missing will be long period swells. Swell periods are only \nexpected to get up to 7 seconds. Will monitor trends for any changes \nto the tide levels and swells.",
